WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.733 --> 00:00:09.032
My name is Mike Rosen and today

00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:10.899
I'm going to tell you about a problem I see

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:12.932
as one of the really great problems today

00:00:12.933 --> 00:00:15.932
in biology. And that is how eukaryotic cells are

00:00:15.933 --> 00:00:17.766
organized on micron length scales.

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:21.532
Now I'll begin by telling you that eukaryotic cells

00:00:21.533 --> 00:00:24.832
are organized across a huge range of length scales.

00:00:24.833 --> 00:00:27.399
And the organization of individual atoms and protein

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.432
molecules to the assembly of those proteins into larger

00:00:30.433 --> 00:00:33.966
and larger complexes, to the organization of those

00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:36.632
complexes into distinct subcellular compartments,

00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:40.632
out to membrane bound organelles in cells, out to

00:00:40.633 --> 00:00:43.566
structures that consist of the entire length of the cell.

00:00:43.567 --> 00:00:47.132
What I'll propose to you is that we understand the

00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:49.932
organization on the shortest and longest length scales

00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:53.332
quite well. So on the shortest scales, we understand a great

00:00:53.333 --> 00:00:57.232
deal about how macromolecules function through

00:00:57.233 --> 00:00:59.866
decades of structural biology and biophysical studies.

00:00:59.867 --> 00:01:03.499
On the longer length scales, we understand through

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.832
cell biological studies that many of the membrane bound

00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:09.266
compartments of the cell, for example, the nucleus,

00:01:09.267 --> 00:01:12.732
the endoplasmic reticulum, golgi apparatus, the mitochondria,

00:01:12.733 --> 00:01:16.299
all of those are very well understood in terms of their structures

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:19.699
and functions. However, there's an intermediate length

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:26.099
scale. Roughly 100 nm to 1 micron, on which we understand

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:28.499
the organization much, much, much more poorly.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:32.532
Because there are in cells many structures of this

00:01:32.533 --> 00:01:36.199
size that are not bound by membranes, that consist of

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.166
tens of thousands to hundreds of thousands of

00:01:39.167 --> 00:01:42.532
individual macromolecules that are involved in many distinct

00:01:42.533 --> 00:01:46.999
areas of biology. Now I'm just showing a short of panel of

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:50.366
those, a representative panel of those structures here.

00:01:50.367 --> 00:01:54.332
So, for example, there are some structures, the JUNQ

00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:56.732
and the IPOD structures that are involved in protein

00:01:56.733 --> 00:01:59.832
quality control. There are structures in the nucleus called

00:01:59.833 --> 00:02:03.532
PML nuclear bodies that are involved in DNA repair.

00:02:03.533 --> 00:02:07.299
There are puncta of various metabolic enzymes that

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:10.299
have been observed in yeast and higher eukaryotes

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:12.932
that are believed to be involved in controlling metabolism.

00:02:12.933 --> 00:02:16.066
There are puncta of numerous signaling molecules,

00:02:16.067 --> 00:02:19.466
such as the disheveled protein here, that are involved

00:02:19.467 --> 00:02:22.766
somehow in mediating signaling activities. There are RNA

00:02:22.767 --> 00:02:25.332
granules of various flavors, for example, these stress

00:02:25.333 --> 00:02:28.032
granules which are involved in translational control.

00:02:28.033 --> 00:02:31.732
There are also numerous structures that exist in

00:02:31.733 --> 00:02:34.832
the domain of the nucleus that are involved in organizing

00:02:34.833 --> 00:02:37.666
of chromatin, which are believed to play an important role

00:02:37.667 --> 00:02:42.066
in gene regulation. So these micron sized membrane-less

00:02:42.067 --> 00:02:45.566
structures are found really throughout all of eukaryotic

00:02:45.567 --> 00:02:50.532
biology. But we understand very little about their functions.

00:02:50.533 --> 00:02:54.066
We understand a lot about the individual molecules that

00:02:54.067 --> 00:02:56.799
control -- that are contained within these structures,

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:00.332
but we don't understand why those molecules, or how

00:03:00.333 --> 00:03:02.799
those molecules are brought together into these micron

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:06.866
sized structures to impart new functionality to cells.

00:03:06.867 --> 00:03:10.999
It turns out that there are also a number of potentially

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.499
analogous structures that are two-dimensional, that is

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.999
clusters of proteins on membranes. And those again

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.299
are important in very larges swathes of biology, and I've

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:24.799
just listed a few here. The immunological synapse, focal

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:28.132
adhesions, invadopodia structures; the initiation of

00:03:28.133 --> 00:03:31.299
invadopodia structures are involved in T-cell activation

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:35.266
cell migration, tumor metastasis. And again, like the

00:03:35.267 --> 00:03:37.932
three-dimensional equivalences, we know very little about

00:03:37.933 --> 00:03:41.732
the nature of these structures and their functions in cells.

00:03:41.733 --> 00:03:48.032
So, there are a series I think of fairly discrete questions

00:03:48.033 --> 00:03:51.166
that we would really like to know about these structures.

00:03:51.167 --> 00:03:54.266
To fill in the gap between macromolecular understanding

00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:58.699
and membrane-bound structure understanding. So

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:01.366
first, what is the physical nature of the structure?

00:04:01.367 --> 00:04:04.632
What are they? What are the molecules that compose them?

00:04:04.633 --> 00:04:07.666
How are those held together to give a discrete structure?

00:04:07.667 --> 00:04:10.999
What are their biochemical and cellular functions

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:15.566
of these cellular structures? And sort of to corollary

00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:19.599
questions, how do the physical properties dictate the

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:22.299
biochemical and cellular functions? And finally, how

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:25.266
can the physical properties, and thus functions,

00:04:25.267 --> 00:04:29.799
be controlled by cells to organize various biological processes?

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:34.832
So, these I think are fairly straightforward set of questions

00:04:34.833 --> 00:04:37.632
but they're all very difficult questions. Let me spend a few

00:04:37.633 --> 00:04:40.332
minutes explaining to you some of the challenges and

00:04:40.333 --> 00:04:42.866
understanding them. And I think a lot of those challenges can

00:04:42.867 --> 00:04:47.199
be illustrated by comparing a macromolecular assembly,

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:50.432
even a very large one like the eukaryotic ribosome, shown

00:04:50.433 --> 00:04:54.266
here, to let's say a generic cellular punctum, these micron

00:04:54.267 --> 00:04:58.032
sized membrane-less structures. So one of the first points

00:04:58.033 --> 00:05:00.166
of comparison that one can make is the size.

00:05:00.167 --> 00:05:03.499
So, even the eukaryotic ribosome, which is a gigantic

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:08.699
macromolecular machine, when you really scale it

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.766
to the size of one of these cellular puncta, it's miniscule.

00:05:11.767 --> 00:05:16.632
So this is the size of a 25nm ribosome would be

00:05:16.633 --> 00:05:21.399
compared to a 1 micron in diameter cellular punctum.

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:25.366
Perhaps even more importantly, though. Not only are these

00:05:25.367 --> 00:05:27.899
much, much smaller, but the size of a macromolecular

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:31.566
complex is fixed by the components that compose it.

00:05:31.567 --> 00:05:34.366
Whereas these cellular puncta, the sizes are highly

00:05:34.367 --> 00:05:37.099
variable, sometimes they'll be small in cells, sometimes

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:40.399
they'll be large. They can transition from large to small

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:43.432
in response to different perturbations. Let me take the

00:05:43.433 --> 00:05:46.832
ribosome back up to an unrealistic size to make my

00:05:46.833 --> 00:05:50.966
additional comparisons. The composition is also very

00:05:50.967 --> 00:05:54.299
different, macromolecular complexes are usually of

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:57.166
fixed stoichiometry such that there's always one copy

00:05:57.167 --> 00:06:00.632
of this protein and one copy of that RNA. However,

00:06:00.633 --> 00:06:03.566
the composition of these cellular puncta is highly

00:06:03.567 --> 00:06:06.732
variable, molecules go in and out of them, some molecules

00:06:06.733 --> 00:06:11.566
stay, some molecules go. They also are very different

00:06:11.567 --> 00:06:14.632
to the level of structure, so macromolecular complexes are

00:06:14.633 --> 00:06:17.532
stereochemically defined across their entire length. So

00:06:17.533 --> 00:06:21.066
if I know where this amino acid is at one end, I know

00:06:21.067 --> 00:06:23.532
the organization of that amino acid at the other end

00:06:23.533 --> 00:06:26.499
of the molecule. However, these cellular puncta are almost

00:06:26.500 --> 00:06:29.699
certainly not defined over their entire lengths. In fact,

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:33.099
we don't know on which length scales they are stereochemically

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:36.099
defined, and over what scale that definition decreases

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:40.466
as one moves out. They also are very different in

00:06:40.467 --> 00:06:43.866
terms of dynamics. Much biochemistry of macromolecular

00:06:43.867 --> 00:06:46.966
machines is governed by fluctuations on microsecond

00:06:46.967 --> 00:06:50.299
and millisecond timescales. Whereas in these cellular

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:53.366
structures, yes the molecules within them move on those

00:06:53.367 --> 00:06:56.499
timescales, but the molecules also move within these

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:59.132
structures and also between these structures and the

00:06:59.133 --> 00:07:01.766
surrounding media on timescales that are much longer,

00:07:01.767 --> 00:07:05.166
seconds to minutes. And frankly, it's really unclear at this

00:07:05.167 --> 00:07:08.332
point which of those timescales of fluctuations are most

00:07:08.333 --> 00:07:10.699
important to the function of these structures.

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:14.532
And finally, I think one of the most important issues

00:07:14.533 --> 00:07:18.132
is function. So we know that macromolecular machines

00:07:18.133 --> 00:07:21.799
function according to the domains that compose them.

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:24.966
Whereas, it's really unclear in these cellular structures

00:07:24.967 --> 00:07:28.532
what functionality can be specifically ascribed to the

00:07:28.533 --> 00:07:31.632
assembly itself and is distinct from the function of the

00:07:31.633 --> 00:07:34.432
individual proteins within them. What does it mean to

00:07:34.433 --> 00:07:36.732
organize them into this much, much larger structure?

00:07:36.733 --> 00:07:38.899
We simply don't know at this point.

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:43.366
And finally, regulation, we know that macromolecular

00:07:43.367 --> 00:07:46.399
machines are regulated often by alterations between

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:49.966
discrete configurations, inactive state and the active

00:07:49.967 --> 00:07:53.332
state. However, because we don't understand the functions

00:07:53.333 --> 00:07:56.766
of these cellular structures, it's unclear really what

00:07:56.767 --> 00:07:59.066
should be controlled. What should we be looking for

00:07:59.067 --> 00:08:01.699
to understand how function is altered by cellular

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:06.766
processes? Now, because of all of these difficulties

00:08:06.767 --> 00:08:10.099
we really need new conceptual frameworks and new

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:13.399
experimental tools to understand these micron-sized

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:16.732
membrane-less cellular structures. Now I'm going to tell you

00:08:16.733 --> 00:08:20.866
over the next few minutes a little bit about some ideas

00:08:20.867 --> 00:08:23.399
that I and others in the field have had about this.

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:27.166
So, there are a couple of potential frameworks, conceptual

00:08:27.167 --> 00:08:30.366
frameworks that derive from polymer science and soft

00:08:30.367 --> 00:08:33.732
matter physics that may play some role here. And

00:08:33.733 --> 00:08:36.632
one of them is this idea of polymerization, because

00:08:36.633 --> 00:08:38.799
most simply, if you think, how do you make a big structure

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:41.466
from a bunch of little structures, the little structures may

00:08:41.467 --> 00:08:44.699
just polymerize to make something much larger.

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:48.399
And when those assembling structures are multivalent,

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:52.699
systems can undergo what's called a cell gel phase

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:56.566
transition, such that in low fractional binding,

00:08:56.567 --> 00:09:00.066
one gets distribution of small complexes. However,

00:09:00.067 --> 00:09:02.599
as fractional binding goes up, there's a very sharp

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:05.632
line beyond which the system becomes capable of

00:09:05.633 --> 00:09:08.999
assembling into huge macromolecular assemblies.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:12.966
And it's possible then, that this soft gel transition

00:09:12.967 --> 00:09:16.099
or polymerization in general, may contribute to these

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:19.699
micron sized structures. Another idea that's been

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:22.999
put forward recently is this notion of phase separations.

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:25.832
So I'm sure everyone's familiar if you mix oil and

00:09:25.833 --> 00:09:28.932
water together and you shake them up, you can get these

00:09:28.933 --> 00:09:31.932
little oil droplets that will float around in aqueous solution.

00:09:31.933 --> 00:09:35.366
Well, it turns out, macromolecules, proteins and RNA,

00:09:35.367 --> 00:09:41.432
in particular, can also do this. And in fact, as a system

00:09:41.433 --> 00:09:44.066
polymerizes, as proteins polymerize they become less

00:09:44.067 --> 00:09:47.599
soluble, they become more able to undergo this

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:49.999
kind of phase separation to produce concentrated

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:53.499
protein droplets, for example, in aqueous solution.

00:09:53.500 --> 00:09:55.766
So again, it's been proposed that some kind of

00:09:55.767 --> 00:09:59.032
phase separation may underlie many of these cellular

00:09:59.033 --> 00:10:02.632
structures. I'm sure though, that with more thought and

00:10:02.633 --> 00:10:06.999
more experiments, there are other conceptual frameworks

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:10.699
that also could be brought to understand these cellular

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:16.699
structures. In terms of tools, the tools that are used

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:20.499
to study macromolecular structures, x-ray crystallography,

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:24.132
NMR, electron microscopy, are really best to describe

00:10:24.133 --> 00:10:27.766
discrete entities or fluctuations between discrete

00:10:27.767 --> 00:10:31.732
conformational states. However, as I told you these cellular

00:10:31.733 --> 00:10:34.066
structures are almost certainly not discrete entities.

00:10:34.067 --> 00:10:37.199
And so we need new tools that will describe them as

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:40.432
distributions of structures, distributions of stoichiometries.

00:10:40.433 --> 00:10:43.799
And probably also tools that will allow us to assess and

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:46.299
perturb the material properties, which may play a role in

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:49.366
the functions. And of course we need ways of relating

00:10:49.367 --> 00:10:52.766
these properties to the individual macromolecular interactions

00:10:52.767 --> 00:10:58.499
that govern their formation. In terms of function,

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:01.066
often in the fields of biophysics and cell biology,

00:11:01.067 --> 00:11:05.766
biochemistry of purified components is used to understand

00:11:05.767 --> 00:11:08.932
mechanistically, the functions of individual molecules

00:11:08.933 --> 00:11:13.366
or individual entities. The problem is that most of these

00:11:13.367 --> 00:11:16.566
cellular structures, in fact, can't be purified. And so we

00:11:16.567 --> 00:11:18.899
need then ways of characterizing their biochemical

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:22.366
properties in situ, or to reconstitute these structures

00:11:22.367 --> 00:11:25.999
from their individual parts. And perhaps most perplexing

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:28.866
at this point is, we need ways of directly interrogating

00:11:28.867 --> 00:11:31.166
the functions of the assemblies themselves.

00:11:31.167 --> 00:11:34.099
Again, as distinct from the components that compose them.

00:11:34.100 --> 00:11:37.166
And we really need new experimental approaches to

00:11:37.167 --> 00:11:41.099
address that issue of assembly. And finally, we need new

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:44.132
theoretical descriptions of these structures. And it may be

00:11:44.133 --> 00:11:47.299
possible to take theories that exist already in polymer

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:50.599
science, colloid science, material science, and apply

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:53.432
them to biology. But an important distinction between

00:11:53.433 --> 00:11:56.232
biology and these other physical sciences is that biology

00:11:56.233 --> 00:11:59.699
is almost always held out away from equilibrium by the

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:02.866
consumption of energy through ATP hydrolysis. And so

00:12:02.867 --> 00:12:06.799
one then needs to take these equilibrium studies and

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:09.966
apply them to biology by incorporating this idea of

00:12:09.967 --> 00:12:14.132
systems away from equilibrium. And I know there are studies

00:12:14.133 --> 00:12:17.299
of active matter in the soft matter physics world that

00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:21.999
may very well be applicable to biology. So, let me just conclude

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:24.499
by coming back to what we would like to know in these systems.

00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:28.832
What are they? What do they do? How does what

00:12:28.833 --> 00:12:31.966
they are dictate what they do? And how can biology

00:12:31.967 --> 00:12:35.532
control all of this to mediate various biological processes?

00:12:35.533 --> 00:12:38.632
So I think if over say, the next decade, we can understand

00:12:38.633 --> 00:12:43.999
all of these questions, we'll fill in this interesting and

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:47.399
confusing gap between macromolecules on one end

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:49.832
and membrane-bound organelles on the other, and

00:12:49.833 --> 00:12:54.066
give us a thorough across scales understanding of biology.

00:12:54.067 --> 00:12:57.366
So hopefully I've inspired some of you to think about this

00:12:57.367 --> 00:12:59.799
problem and hopefully you, over the course of your career,

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:01.832
will be able to answer many of these questions.

00:13:01.833 --> 00:13:03.367
Thank you.

